WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.220
Thanks to Brilliant for helping support this episode.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:04.460
Hey Crazies.

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:06.720
There are a lot of stars in the universe.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.300
Somewhere around 10-to-the-24.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:13.020
That’s a 1 followed by 24 zeros.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.660
I’m not even going to try to makes sense of that.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:16.760
It’s big.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.900
And those stars come in all the basic colors

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:22.000
except green and purple.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:29.580
This episode was made possible by generous supporters on Patreon.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:34.280
See. A star’s color comes from one of its outermost layers.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.260
A layer called the chromosphere, or color sphere.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.800
Because, you know, science names.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.580
The light emitted from the chromosphere is based on its temperature,

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:49.040
which means we can tell a stars surface temperature just by its color.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:50.700
How cool is that?!

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:55.240
Red stars are the coolest and blue stars are the hottest.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:59.100
I know this is backward from the labels you see on sink handles and stuff.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.340
Those colors are thermodynamically inaccurate.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.500
Where was I again? Right. Stars.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:09.040
In temperature order, they go from red to blue.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.920
But, where we'd expect to have a green star, we have a white star instead.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.080
Why though?

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:17.480
Statistical mechanics!

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:22.220
An object’s temperature comes the wiggling and jiggling of its little particles.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:27.320
In fact, we relate that temperature directly to the kinetic energy of those particles.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.140
That’s the energy of their motion, their wiggling and jiggling.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:36.000
As the particles jiggle around, they bump into each other and lose some of that energy,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.600
which is carried away as light.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.700
But each individual particle is jiggling a different amount.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.540
This is only the average kinetic energy of the particles.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:50.520
The actual kinetic energy of any given particle probably isn’t that number.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:54.260
What did you expect? The universe to be simple? Pffft!

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:58.920
When they bump into each other, different particles will lose different amounts of energy

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:01.760
and, therefore, release different light.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:05.340
Statistically, the distribution looks something like this.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.640
We call it a black body curve.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:13.220
This is what the curve looks like for the Sun and it matches up well with experimental data.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:16.300
But, we can’t actually see all of that light.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:20.740
We only see a tiny fraction of it, what we call visible light.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.940
Because, you know, science names.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:25.540
They’re boring, but descriptive.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:29.380
Seriously though, the scale on this graphic isn’t linear.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:31.220
It’s a logarithmic scale.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:35.700
Some people call it exponential scale, but don’t get me started on another rant.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.900
On a chart that goes from a trillionth of a meter up to 10 kilometers.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:44.060
The visible range only spans 300 nanometers.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:48.280
If you were to stretch the full spectrum between Los Angeles and New York,

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:52.720
the visible part would only be as wide as this single piece of thread.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:54.400
Human eyes suck.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.580
Anyway, let’s get back to that black body curve.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:00.460
We don’t need the entire curve to understand color.

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:02.860
We only need the part inside the visible range.

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:04.780
We can’t see the rest anyway.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:08.880
Since different colors are emitted simultaneously, they’ll mix together

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.280
and we have to worry about what that’s going to look like to human eyes.

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:16.160
We saw in a previous video how human vision actually works.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.760
Our eyes have red, blue, and green sensors that send signals to the brain.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:25.340
Our brain combines those signals to interpret the entire visible spectrum.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:29.760
But it has an interpretation for every possible signal it could receive.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:33.920
Including signals that don’t correspond to a single wavelength of light.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:37.920
To understand color, we need chromaticity graph.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:43.640
This assigns a color to every possible signal the human brain could receive from the eyes.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:47.190
Actually, an RGB screen can’t display all those colors.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:49.440
He’s right. I’m lying a little bit.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:50.200
Mmhmm.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:55.960
Technically, an RGB screen can only accurately display colors inside this triangle.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:58.680
Outside of that triangle, it’s faking it.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.760
It’s the best I can do, ok?!

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:02.380
So, chromaticity.

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:07.700
This outer curved edge is all the colors that corresponds to a single wavelength of light,

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:09.980
what we call monochromatic colors

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:12.200
or single-color colors.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:15.160
Again, boring name, but descriptive.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:19.480
Anyway, that curve is the entire visible spectrum.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:23.860
From red all the way up to violet or what you might call purple.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:27.920
There isn’t much purple though, only a tiny bit at the very end.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:31.860
Everything else on the chart is a combination of multiple colors.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:34.280
That’s where you’ll find the rest of the purple.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:37.680
So let’s see what happens with the light coming from a star.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.160
Red stars aren’t too complicated.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:44.020
Almost all the light is red, so the star appears red.

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:49.620
As we look at hotter stars, the color shifts as we’d expect; orange and then yellow,

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:53.040
but something weird happens when that peak shifts to green.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.760
Even though it’s peaking in green, there are a lot of other colors coming from that star.

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:00.240
There might be more green than anything else,

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:03.900
but there’s a significant amount of all visible wavelengths.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:09.280
There’s also a roughly equal amount coming from the blue side as there is from the red side.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:12.060
The monochromatic colors are mixing together.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.180
Back to the chromaticity graph!

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:17.280
Star colors aren’t along the monochromatic curve.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:19.060
They’re along this curve.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:21.140
We call it color temperature.

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:24.260
You'll find the coolest stars in the red corner.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:26.500
I’m ready coach!

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:29.320
Oh, no, I didn’t mean that kind of red corner.

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:31.719
Man! It’s been forever since I’ve been in the ring!

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:35.400
I know. I’m sorry. I'll find a use for you soon. I promise.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:36.740
Just put me in!

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:42.140
Ok, so the coolest stars are in the red spot.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:44.340
They’re nearly monochromatic.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:48.220
As we look at hotter stars, we get orange and yellow.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.960
But they’re not monochromatic orange and yellow.

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:53.940
Color mixing gradually gets more extreme.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:06:00.760
By the time we expect to see green, there are so many other colors around that the star looks white instead.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:05.520
All colors mixed together in roughly equal amounts looks like white to us,

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:07.360
so we don’t get any green stars.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:09.480
We get white ones like our Sun.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:11.720
Yes, the Sun is white.

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:16.460
This balance between the colors disappears when we look at even hotter stars.

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:20.360
As the temperature goes up, the peak continues moving to the left.

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:24.820
That means more blue light and less red light will be emitted from the star.

00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:30.320
If the star is hot enough, that imbalance will be large enough for the star to look blue.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:32.220
So how hot to be a purple star?

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:33.760
Never going to happen.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:35.780
Seriously. Never.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:40.680
That black body curve might be shifting to the left, but it's also going up.

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:46.580
It might be peaking in ultraviolet or x-rays or something, but there’s still a lot of visible light.

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:49.840
and that intense mix of colors keeps the star blue,

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:52.400
no matter what the surface temperature is.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:56.560
The color temperature on the chromaticity graph dead ends in blue.

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:00.040
I mean, stars can’t get anywhere near infinite surface temperature.

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:04.120
The hottest stars only get up to about 200 thousand kelvin,

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:05.960
but you get the point.

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:08.440
So why aren’t there green or purple stars?

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:12.180
Because color is more complicated than the visible spectrum.

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:16.540
Stars emit multiple wavelengths of light at the same time.

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:22.560
When colors mix, we have to consider how all those signals are going to be interpreted by the human brain.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:28.600
Stars emit light based on their surface temperature, which only generates colors along this curve.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.580
Green and purple aren’t on that curve.

00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:34.880
So how cool would it be to see green and purple stars anyway?

